[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048175 :topic/id #uuid "85bac388-4397-5418-87a6-a1048f67cdda" :topic/math-academy-id 2130 :topic/title "Type I, II, and III Regions in Three-Dimensional Space"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbac9d6d-45d4-5136-bf5b-083c2757b419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460179} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c459cf-e47e-5a6b-9306-a1204647fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c459cf-e47e-5a6b-9306-a1204647fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460180} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b856b25-5fd9-5622-a242-7cea8720d0d6" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a637e26-81c3-5744-ad03-798d29b7967e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460181} {:answer/id #uuid "09f35204-c8ab-52a4-bd7d-3608868a9ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a637e26-81c3-5744-ad03-798d29b7967e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460181} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93bf86b-d526-5213-848a-da369e8a84ca" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3a351d-1467-5ea7-bd72-80d0b233aa70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460183} {:answer/id #uuid "27e6529b-e064-5869-8608-e8d719d6e58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a3a351d-1467-5ea7-bd72-80d0b233aa70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460183} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb947e93-9b13-5cf0-a1cf-785b08e6926d" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddcaae1f-cad0-5e0d-8af5-aa602e286e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-73574" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a266f4d3ab4254684c91cb422f96b70a4c9973f587b6afb2cd7af5db45e86561.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $z=4-x-y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$” and statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$” are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](images/cd/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526 :topic/title "Forced Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3fb80e-bfa7-5017-a56d-069fd980c629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460221} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbb63c5-39b6-5a60-b691-07641148a928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccbb63c5-39b6-5a60-b691-07641148a928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460222} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d99343-4b79-5f95-9d4b-e725afec1d20" :answer-field/key "$y$ $={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7c0bf6-51dc-5572-a384-70e76b7378a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460223} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0fbdb1-55c8-5908-99aa-c9f1404ab684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b7c0bf6-51dc-5572-a384-70e76b7378a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460223} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f1c2b1a-f87f-5559-a10d-b8e0fa87b72d" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A{e}^{-3t}$ $+B{e}^{4t}$ $+17\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460978} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d55bea-a33e-52fd-80aa-c6b6b76ff7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460225} {:answer/id #uuid "983462b8-1f79-5e9f-8972-909efa066fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "983462b8-1f79-5e9f-8972-909efa066fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460226} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca0fcf8a-28f2-50c1-8bf7-172d804d702a" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9aa670fe-3757-54df-85dc-a7b3def25d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79461" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $y$ $={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y$ $=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y$ $={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$; $y$ $=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e77d06e-75de-5efc-a31a-08ab5cd28827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460125} {:answer/id #uuid "d7273db4-f030-554b-9eb4-1f8ad7088e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7273db4-f030-554b-9eb4-1f8ad7088e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460126} :answer-field/id #uuid "7255451a-6f73-5904-b058-7ec744515ab6" :answer-field/key "$x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y$ $={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1476747-7cd7-5321-b84b-6e1235b6e7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460127} {:answer/id #uuid "f91c9644-913e-582a-adaf-b144e1e7ea1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1476747-7cd7-5321-b84b-6e1235b6e7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460127} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14339dc-b2b5-545f-aa5f-519565543cd4" :answer-field/key "${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da02e26-cd27-5a84-95fc-65e7c16865c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460129} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f92fd3-cff0-5229-b161-7053c1989dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da02e26-cd27-5a84-95fc-65e7c16865c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460129} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf992f83-6c65-58e9-a6aa-5bc334f98c34" :answer-field/key "${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y$ $=0,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c0602e3-c50d-5991-b1fc-f704c082ddef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65014" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99d976a-f891-5006-b006-1f89b0aed82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460261} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9e8db1-87d7-5816-a338-04d02f84dbe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9e8db1-87d7-5816-a338-04d02f84dbe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460262} :answer-field/id #uuid "8797be38-2755-5b03-9566-c909c3af6f17" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3bef40b-07d1-567e-a53c-086d7570c681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460263} {:answer/id #uuid "561e321f-7bb3-54a3-9484-d79887120eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bef40b-07d1-567e-a53c-086d7570c681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460263} :answer-field/id #uuid "632f4f21-76a6-593f-87d5-e2ea2f58cd86" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0bf614e-4003-5fba-acfe-eac707b4783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460265} {:answer/id #uuid "537369b8-b99a-5260-b918-86b0e348c6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0bf614e-4003-5fba-acfe-eac707b4783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5cb38e4-a3c2-56e0-91a3-3fb1f913c942" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f66ee2a6-cdd7-5b19-895e-1ea6e9717b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80267" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/76/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce78fae-2f75-53fa-95bd-89e42404c306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477479} {:answer/id #uuid "1b24b138-1fa1-567a-831b-225ce9fec150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce78fae-2f75-53fa-95bd-89e42404c306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477479} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01afced-5cb8-5e2c-931d-27b4d81cd2e2" :answer-field/key "$(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53988cad-d318-5f3f-bf57-d4bc54928a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477481} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bd54da-91bd-5c85-808a-ca8ed4c159bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53988cad-d318-5f3f-bf57-d4bc54928a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477481} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08f93ed-7a64-5792-b5f3-6d063d76e508" :answer-field/key "$8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y$ $=0,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=10$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a80a224-6a08-5039-8d41-f5bdc35d47b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477483} {:answer/id #uuid "3933d0c9-4404-59c0-9d7f-9189bf388be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3933d0c9-4404-59c0-9d7f-9189bf388be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477484} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b137a8b-1bb9-50f9-aa84-f3a138116b17" :answer-field/key "${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}$ $={x}^{2}+1,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b755a996-db20-51e4-9f08-2f14dd2e4212" :question/math-academy-id "q-93952" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049554 :topic/id #uuid "fed99c84-950a-5788-8cb4-d3c785359494" :topic/math-academy-id 2815 :topic/title "Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486718-90fd-5e9e-9943-9c86b1e6e1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477443} {:answer/id #uuid "41dc14f9-6472-581d-834c-b92f0b3b2bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad486718-90fd-5e9e-9943-9c86b1e6e1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477443} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df7f439-9445-5270-ae1a-891e7120f5d2" :answer-field/key "$n=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b060754-d061-59ec-a9a4-7d3e81891ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477445} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcd7196-0244-50a4-8bdb-048788557e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b060754-d061-59ec-a9a4-7d3e81891ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477445} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ecb0617-9757-55bc-89dc-7a801657e9ca" :answer-field/key "$0>1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477692} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f75710-65ee-5b76-9a06-ee5a8f0faada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477447} {:answer/id #uuid "aae597d2-48dc-5c42-8b53-4cd34676f0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae597d2-48dc-5c42-8b53-4cd34676f0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477448} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb65f1bb-be9e-535c-b822-5ff99fd81495" :answer-field/key "${n}^{2}\\ge 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59712b43-1263-520b-8160-c638c0e80547" :question/math-academy-id "q-80085" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $n>2,$ then $\\underline{\\hspace{3em}}.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overbrace{Q}^{\\text{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate $n=0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement $0>1$ is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f828cb-c8b1-5da4-9711-15ace712dffa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Vacuous Statement" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049554 :topic/id #uuid "fed99c84-950a-5788-8cb4-d3c785359494" :topic/math-academy-id 2815 :topic/title "Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fabe7482-fcde-52a8-b0a8-c3faff096f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477449} {:answer/id #uuid "24926cac-849b-539c-a163-5ab2b0e89411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fabe7482-fcde-52a8-b0a8-c3faff096f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477449} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3820eb2-ae82-5f8f-b87f-b54c051d2cd9" :answer-field/key "$n<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcfede58-069c-56a2-a244-b1d515c7fd86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477451} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e556c-c357-5d2b-a246-a1af5c0ff416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0e556c-c357-5d2b-a246-a1af5c0ff416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477452} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6667cbc-d7db-5c51-aeb4-22f633e0f554" :answer-field/key "$0>1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477695} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8085c2e-7d1f-5d36-8fbe-bcf4bd3c6777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477453} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8bf7f5-cb88-50ce-8714-fc5ebd01b4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c8bf7f5-cb88-50ce-8714-fc5ebd01b4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1313c6a-e792-5e4a-9bf3-227ee46641a1" :answer-field/key "${n}^{2}<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb09ac10-eb5c-5627-9b3a-f58793f9c5b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80089" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $\\underline{\\hspace{3em}},$ then $n>0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overbrace{P}^{\\text{Always false}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate $n<0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement $0>1$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate ${n}^{2}<0$, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $0>1$; ${n}^{2}<0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048 :topic/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d11452-10c2-5831-9ed7-380e969e7b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448392} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1a66bb-528f-5ae9-a6b6-baf32fc68f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c1a66bb-528f-5ae9-a6b6-baf32fc68f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448393} :answer-field/id #uuid "44c3a11b-946f-59b6-a34a-3acdec238eeb" :answer-field/key "$P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "511b8338-3e84-5027-bdba-5f783909c79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448394} {:answer/id #uuid "92717367-d7e6-58ad-b6b9-3cf0901ac6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92717367-d7e6-58ad-b6b9-3cf0901ac6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448395} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfeed298-ac80-5dfc-807e-f2a4889da113" :answer-field/key "$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b165c252-960d-5ef2-b499-ac9b46df0c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448396} {:answer/id #uuid "b969839b-38f3-56cf-9041-dd861a2a1354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b969839b-38f3-56cf-9041-dd861a2a1354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448397} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a5af12-58bd-55c1-9f30-2f617724855c" :answer-field/key "$X$ and $Y$ are independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25122d3d-9cb1-5f5e-b327-d0566bb4de5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-136853" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$ is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is true. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$; “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$”; “$X$ and $Y$ are independent”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048 :topic/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87397041-9730-5ac2-a54f-4f891bebe983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459599} {:answer/id #uuid "81a4679b-33f0-5db9-bcd4-8c2fa9f080a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87397041-9730-5ac2-a54f-4f891bebe983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459599} :answer-field/id #uuid "98585a00-8503-5452-8f06-692b91a8edad" :answer-field/key "$P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460666} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1a0a1d9-e9bd-523d-979a-8498255ba1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459601} {:answer/id #uuid "6922f743-73d0-5a55-99be-0d69f049e344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1a0a1d9-e9bd-523d-979a-8498255ba1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459601} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a67bd3-f3d9-56ab-8352-c30b3a171ccd" :answer-field/key "$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe8f777-32ed-5e0c-81b1-d151b12d83b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459603} {:answer/id #uuid "9c039497-425d-51d2-ac3d-12ab757783ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe8f777-32ed-5e0c-81b1-d151b12d83b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459603} :answer-field/id #uuid "39be9191-3d03-583a-8f67-fb10524c2446" :answer-field/key "$X$ and $Y$ are independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b10e877-0140-5f79-97b2-25494c500279" :question/math-academy-id "q-162744" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements $P(X=1,Y=1)$ $=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$ and “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is false. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/id #uuid "02ecb448-e544-51fa-b80e-63ee84a4cef1" :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1657ec-ee94-56ab-9624-eb98b93a3f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422339 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba78363-9c7b-5138-bdae-fcc1c1e8b161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1657ec-ee94-56ab-9624-eb98b93a3f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422339 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "91464ac2-012a-5628-9de2-8468b2c1d708" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}$ $=3{y}^{′},$ $\\,y(1)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baf4f4e-994c-5a3d-8cd7-a08d6815e9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422341} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77a9c5-67ca-5442-a2b6-728548b85c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422342 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca77a9c5-67ca-5442-a2b6-728548b85c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422342 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "df06a3a0-7e3c-5b74-b120-cc16d360e1b5" :answer-field/key "$2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9b50315-f8d9-5f53-a15e-c35152183cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422343 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} {:answer/id #uuid "00362934-c6f7-5519-a9c5-57cedf485a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b50315-f8d9-5f53-a15e-c35152183cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422343 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "41909812-23d6-5023-aa6c-ce2d4a23c2b5" :answer-field/key "$4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}$ $=3{y}^{′},$ $\\,y(1)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=1$ is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$ only." :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409286 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 18.089 :task-item/id #uuid "af5ea72f-851a-4b53-8ec3-48d3b87dfc01" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409287} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1657ec-ee94-56ab-9624-eb98b93a3f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422339} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77a9c5-67ca-5442-a2b6-728548b85c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422342} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b50315-f8d9-5f53-a15e-c35152183cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422343}] :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412 :topic/id #uuid "46db30fb-cff1-5fef-85ad-641fbb5c68f2" :topic/math-academy-id 3343 :topic/title "Vector Fields"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db2c395-ec42-5ded-b118-40b7f641733e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437612} {:answer/id #uuid "416808ed-a781-5b4b-95e5-19a652e061b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416808ed-a781-5b4b-95e5-19a652e061b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437613} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b88c55-88f1-525f-b310-4ec0ae74fb86" :answer-field/key "$P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b870f025-7855-54e1-ba52-ff6dc5d12029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437614} {:answer/id #uuid "272a82e6-92c9-5b61-b5b0-91bb28d461e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b870f025-7855-54e1-ba52-ff6dc5d12029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437614} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd800195-cb3a-5cb6-83d8-4a289879ed7b" :answer-field/key "$Q(x,y,z)$ $=y\\,i$ $-xy\\,j$ $+(z+1)\\,k$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d35aa822-dcce-5b60-823d-bc8fd4d47fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437616} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad27bc-d96f-5039-a317-bdadedc1b542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d35aa822-dcce-5b60-823d-bc8fd4d47fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437616} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b9cc2d-8a9d-5c85-8be1-4e96ec3dbdef" :answer-field/key "$R(t)=(t+1)\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b23ed53-bb63-5272-af53-a6429d08e4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122643" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412 :topic/id #uuid "46db30fb-cff1-5fef-85ad-641fbb5c68f2" :topic/math-academy-id 3343 :topic/title "Vector Fields"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662120df-b64d-54ef-9755-4c19a2619b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437690} {:answer/id #uuid "5f979ab0-c9f1-5a39-8279-36823d8a4b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f979ab0-c9f1-5a39-8279-36823d8a4b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437691} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a1bfb0-97af-571d-8fa7-cabca9f81b58" :answer-field/key "$U(x,y,z)$ $=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c998f6-9652-545f-9f75-a13c62365a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437692} {:answer/id #uuid "b439a47b-3c58-5ada-b83b-cb0d5f96aba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c998f6-9652-545f-9f75-a13c62365a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437692} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e0a766-53e1-58da-b795-ea4aa130a9a2" :answer-field/key "$V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a15fd7a1-495c-5a1f-9e2c-3e2cf8f73c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437694} {:answer/id #uuid "c163aff7-0957-5238-8912-1448c3c67129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a15fd7a1-495c-5a1f-9e2c-3e2cf8f73c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437694} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec68c1f1-2b47-5081-9d50-db69af906f2a" :answer-field/key "$W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4052d0d0-adca-5eb3-9bef-49295b1e5651" :question/math-academy-id "q-122697" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $U(x,y,z)$ $=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140e8026-dca4-518e-b2e3-6a5204699c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448696} {:answer/id #uuid "91598bc4-014d-5d6b-96f5-a46fe7a19ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91598bc4-014d-5d6b-96f5-a46fe7a19ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448697} :answer-field/id #uuid "b185c70a-936c-56ce-a09e-491c0f5906d5" :answer-field/key "If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f7fce4d-3fa7-56db-8465-e02eebbb3df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448698} {:answer/id #uuid "973d8243-55d9-5ba6-9a8d-9dc8eca9d0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f7fce4d-3fa7-56db-8465-e02eebbb3df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448698} :answer-field/id #uuid "6276cdd8-8c2d-5458-9242-9db53bf98878" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8433f050-2198-5d04-bcdc-9660fdcf23e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448700} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8ade48-b616-5c30-ace3-11755e23e2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8433f050-2198-5d04-bcdc-9660fdcf23e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448700} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfb10db-820c-5886-b42e-f40a5038d34d" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b3a8f74-a4d2-5020-9ea6-4077acac1b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47524" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible” is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Im}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,3x+2y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406809} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406847}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57d2ca69-fe95-54f0-aad8-443617662a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459347} {:answer/id #uuid "de690490-72bf-5c90-a293-08435eace5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de690490-72bf-5c90-a293-08435eace5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e355aa-15a4-5a22-b9e5-e88f52e20aae" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460540} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c9609f-e55a-5da3-a3dd-a8cd7b2f8d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459349} {:answer/id #uuid "bc157483-6944-5715-8eb9-987ffd92b338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c9609f-e55a-5da3-a3dd-a8cd7b2f8d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459349} :answer-field/id #uuid "7613d32b-c5ca-5ef1-9d27-8bbf082c4ac4" :answer-field/key "$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<5\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1b039b-b084-53b0-96f0-7271425ca3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459351} {:answer/id #uuid "822ec6e5-7d2e-5a73-b1e9-337eefabcaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822ec6e5-7d2e-5a73-b1e9-337eefabcaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459352} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcbcd2d-e013-5f8c-a9fa-537b5994c968" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05445495-e773-5bcb-8190-bfc30cf9688f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105211" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le x+1,\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e0b353-6c44-5848-84f3-ca21bd8a12f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c29d621-f793-5227-8c45-cdc973193a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c63b21-f398-5ff0-8619-174f250b0c41" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} {:answer/id #uuid "50f64642-889e-55ea-970c-b9b08f2e4f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313ba5ba-4b76-5ce8-89d7-ca607ead8684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459855} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5befb55-bf65-517b-b35a-8ddfd6d9c0c0" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1\\le {x}^{2}\\le 9,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac6fd3d-18ec-57f8-aeb6-c69b06f785dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459857} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0d506b5-c66a-532f-bcab-23809b19c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459858} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6fc26cd-9cd3-5000-bc44-9e01a45d4536" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff2c4e7d-b661-5abc-a5ef-3c554262f85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35862" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,1<y<4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc67ad68-9c4e-5e01-ad45-5e0583b64cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477247} {:answer/id #uuid "33373e69-3a76-5ea7-a9ae-d867253e930d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33373e69-3a76-5ea7-a9ae-d867253e930d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477248} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a91c133-f09c-5414-8406-55eccccaf1d1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4347200-6d55-5c61-a030-a4ac871e9a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477249} {:answer/id #uuid "18b92bb6-72b5-59f3-9611-f5668d921023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4347200-6d55-5c61-a030-a4ac871e9a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477249} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3901499-272d-57fb-ac61-d587813aa8eb" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2,}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ea91e4-3c46-5e6e-a294-087688f785fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477251} {:answer/id #uuid "0aff04e8-7fae-5878-94e2-41f564a32c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ea91e4-3c46-5e6e-a294-087688f785fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477251} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67f297c-e6e9-5e72-a41b-b78ae9b4d08e" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\\\\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8166eb05-ff92-507f-8eb2-6ecb8cda44b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35866" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed