 have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049324 :topic/id #uuid "ebff858d-86f0-5d5d-b497-f8fb2d20cce6" :topic/math-academy-id 2742 :topic/title "Introduction to Boundary Value Problems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af26650-ec92-5825-8bf7-ee6389246739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc23aaf2-cd82-5980-a662-d5c724c0a3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af26650-ec92-5825-8bf7-ee6389246739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463910} :answer-field/id #uuid "26cc8fec-a525-553b-84c7-2a49f9f7a723" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+y=x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8050b864-d756-570c-bd55-2d6a32259063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463912} {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e5f25d-f44f-5e9b-a0ce-a0a2af2176a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463913} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf4a2dd-ef1c-5f26-ad2d-413c7be0f7cc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}$ $=xy,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=3,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f248fc35-c661-5a21-bea0-6e5c93c92540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463914} {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639ef54f-38f0-5daa-b3e3-33ab6892a11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463915} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b143b8-d1db-5fb6-b810-dd1742976cc5" :answer-field/key "$2{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e2ad896-b5d8-56df-9f2a-960d9cc29ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130997" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} {:answer/id #uuid "12f38159-dc63-5fba-849a-fd4cbddd37a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6072c7e5-1b9e-53ce-b826-0c4a3ff90a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e220417c-1d48-5050-a73f-0084d4f70153" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y$ $=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f55f435-e3f7-5996-93ac-f561dbe5c765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463918} {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56b3f3f-19de-51b4-b8a9-fc77eab7606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463919} :answer-field/id #uuid "69952d04-741c-5c07-a22e-2cc948d39226" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y$ $=1,$ $\\,y(\\pi )$ $=1,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdfa32f-e041-5523-8c32-8b0bb4b269a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3b12fe-cd05-5467-8f10-72a36bc176df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6309537b-a09e-5bac-8903-1a1e4188fddb" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d793473-d655-59e4-94f6-f213bc81d597" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048 :topic/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c0be41-4842-5d84-9243-b20970ca65f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464270} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3a1711-1ce6-584c-8666-fd81316fdd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3a1711-1ce6-584c-8666-fd81316fdd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464271} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e8b9f1-2710-5c4d-97d6-670c3ce72f83" :answer-field/key "$P(X=2,Y=1)$ $=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6dc7de0-fb70-572e-9395-bf45e4359ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464272} {:answer/id #uuid "3e54788f-563d-5897-822c-4ffcb07e00ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e54788f-563d-5897-822c-4ffcb07e00ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464273} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82999e5-03fb-56c4-8ba2-aa587b618145" :answer-field/key "$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a65e48-8a48-5658-be41-145c5aab2fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464274} {:answer/id #uuid "3d42055b-43bb-5621-bee4-f237edcf65e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d42055b-43bb-5621-bee4-f237edcf65e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464275} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d508279-cf49-5ea0-a9cb-97c308df61f8" :answer-field/key "$X$ and $Y$ are independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a347e7c2-3db8-5058-926f-eeec20d91744" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P(X=2,Y=1)$ $=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$ is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is true. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(X=2,Y=1)$ $=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$; “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$”; “$X$ and $Y$ are independent”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048 :topic/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05112f38-a021-5475-adbd-e2dab978dcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464276} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e27b73-a786-50ad-befd-9af1bceb49a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05112f38-a021-5475-adbd-e2dab978dcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464276} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd056097-b408-58b6-9d07-0013368a4eac" :answer-field/key "$P(X=2,Y=2)$ $=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2dc0905-947a-5502-afb0-e31f99420f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464278} {:answer/id #uuid "11f19573-031c-5a4e-92e5-69affffb8f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2dc0905-947a-5502-afb0-e31f99420f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464278} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c916fa-571a-52d8-9c46-9dc13d9b2bdd" :answer-field/key "$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd7ef690-8b54-58fe-9a21-0a4293d10ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464280} {:answer/id #uuid "4edd5db2-ceaa-577d-84c0-e2e7014339d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd7ef690-8b54-58fe-9a21-0a4293d10ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464280} :answer-field/id #uuid "793fa71c-2507-582d-aece-7b85ad9d5350" :answer-field/key "$X$ and $Y$ are independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e48e8cd-fab9-56d8-9673-3c5737feeff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143333" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements $P(X=2,Y=2)$ $=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$ and “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=2)=f(2,2)=0.4.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=2,Y=2)\\ne P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement “$X$ and $Y$ are independent” is false. Since statement “$P(X=x,Y=y)$ $=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$” is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048 :topic/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e75e0302-d864-58b2-85e3-94ee824dd2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464282} {:answer/id #uuid "81aa670d-7b50-5dbc-876a-7211f2efdce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e75e0302-d864-58b2-85e3-94ee824dd2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value 