y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{2}(y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $+{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $+{e}^{x}y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707 :topic/title "Linear Differential Operators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8462488e-272b-5772-bf64-9aa1638edb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466405} {:answer/id #uuid "06728173-b51c-55c4-92d8-9851289d57bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06728173-b51c-55c4-92d8-9851289d57bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466406} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa1bd4a-3eba-56e1-a3ba-6e63da10f9e6" :answer-field/key "${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc57e6f5-5ce9-51db-a506-35f8fe3453e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466407} {:answer/id #uuid "634f6c08-51bc-5474-b7ad-cca79735d829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "634f6c08-51bc-5474-b7ad-cca79735d829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466408} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33b4fc0-086e-5fab-be31-3e3d8f7b2025" :answer-field/key "${D}_{2}(y)$ $=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $-2y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e38e3054-6ed9-56c7-8585-41217d71f832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466409} {:answer/id #uuid "1168cf38-73e5-5de7-a26b-6306956051c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1168cf38-73e5-5de7-a26b-6306956051c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466410} :answer-field/id #uuid "10a51316-22f5-5b3e-9744-bdf00aa12c02" :answer-field/key "${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a6ed137-ea67-591f-bf1a-a0d1b0037ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337778" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$; ${D}_{2}(y)$ $=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $-2y$; ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707 :topic/title "Linear Differential Operators"} :activity/title "Linear Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433323} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433345}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707 :topic/title "Linear Differential Operators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537e9287-d40b-541a-a771-35fdebb7a68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466417} {:answer/id #uuid "b86f20e9-e99e-5043-a105-41ba0d06d907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537e9287-d40b-541a-a771-35fdebb7a68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466417} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee149c43-cd32-54e1-88b1-1a761ba1088d" :answer-field/key "${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ff9540-7748-56d0-8dcc-17c53b286037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466419} {:answer/id #uuid "99c15a2b-52b5-5f4f-a9bd-e392ccd7dd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ff9540-7748-56d0-8dcc-17c53b286037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466419} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbafeabb-acd0-55ae-b50b-63a06106240a" :answer-field/key "${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71c453fc-3161-5efe-87c9-b5487086f7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466421} {:answer/id #uuid "d3699871-4a43-5354-8111-f56a66a66074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3699871-4a43-5354-8111-f56a66a66074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466422} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b0a69c-b01a-51bf-ad6d-bd58d94109aa" :answer-field/key "${D}_{3}(y)$ $=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1c5937c-0d4d-5848-8380-65f08dc10e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-338104" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{3}(y)$ $=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ $+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ $+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a39e901-0aa1-5d86-b708-0e8acb2ac5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466423} {:answer/id #uuid "29e4116f-8a15-53fe-9ea6-b1b9dad75542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a39e901-0aa1-5d86-b708-0e8acb2ac5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466423} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9207047-339f-5590-b907-7f7c565f9552" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(0)$ $=1,$ $\\,y(2)$ $=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865dc4fa-d649-5eb9-a7ec-5bde925f9d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466425} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaaf4f2-b503-5bf6-83a4-be3c5e2cef67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcaaf4f2-b503-5bf6-83a4-be3c5e2cef67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466426} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c825d3a-0580-5864-b085-36ece98d3299" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+4y$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6959ade9-49ae-5973-9caa-620065c11d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466427} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc34701-51c2-57e9-8294-7861a2a09033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6959ade9-49ae-5973-9caa-620065c11d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466427} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b83349-428f-55e7-acb7-eb348ac4f370" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dabe6837-4031-5ea3-85c8-7a53d65944dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-338531" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677 :topic/id #uuid "5e132315-9606-5b30-bee3-61f0209d40c4" :topic/math-academy-id 2548 :topic/title "Introduction to Second-Order Linear ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6836982-f6d8-52f8-815d-9525a8903655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466429} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0a38e7-67c6-52ec-be17-5586868af018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6836982-f6d8-52f8-815d-9525a8903655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466429} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f230617-a629-5dbf-9fa6-42a16dd6c5bc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=2,$ $\\,y(5)$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04973466-3738-5bff-b1f2-ace9af564b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466431} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8e13eb-9e6f-58ad-a231-0486e26e30f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04973466-3738-5bff-b1f2-ace9af564b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466431} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2885e5d-2c03-53c7-bc3d-19f929c42aac" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe77deb-cfc5-5881-8d56-96c7bcafedbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466433} {:answer/id #uuid "a39275f9-b508-525c-a97a-9587ce4361a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39275f9-b508-525c-a97a-9587ce4361a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466434} :answer-field/id #uuid "750c2223-91a4-5d91-ae4f-93359625a42b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(-3)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(-3)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce074393-6c5e-5e9b-ab9a-92fd971d7d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049324 :topic/id #uuid "ebff858d-86f0-5d5d-b497-f8fb2d20cce6" :topic/math-academy-id 2742 :topic/title "Introduction to Boundary Value Problems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974d1fce-aeff-5a83-83a9-3bb949b0b70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466435} {:answer/id #uuid "f12d0b9d-f226-5f1e-921e-a2e48e09c132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f12