ype/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467248} {:answer/id #uuid "2528c821-b847-54f0-8883-6d9ed55b4f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2528c821-b847-54f0-8883-6d9ed55b4f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467249} :answer-field/id #uuid "918b7c5a-dd99-5cbf-b43f-6939bcb541bb" :answer-field/key "$C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92efc5e4-6fcf-5867-b774-9cceef4cda9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34327" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$; $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91737558-6c05-5704-a1bd-97256ba8ac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467250} {:answer/id #uuid "d54efb51-dae9-51e7-b753-b49c7cc205e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d54efb51-dae9-51e7-b753-b49c7cc205e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467251} :answer-field/id #uuid "3550657e-00e5-5321-84f3-a02f359e43d1" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e027dfef-3f17-5a98-aa1a-cd6ebb8d1099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467252} {:answer/id #uuid "3c812a6a-0c0e-5d30-a276-7f0ed6bd7075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c812a6a-0c0e-5d30-a276-7f0ed6bd7075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467253} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4abc7d-91c0-59ab-bd90-0b73bea7fd8e" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2ae926-4da0-5141-8140-53b3f5f56040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467254} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2a20f6-f926-52e2-8658-01e2e788fd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2ae926-4da0-5141-8140-53b3f5f56040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467254} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf5bb9c-c605-5d0f-b975-f7f042212076" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c611f45-045c-5502-9311-b6daf9203ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343739" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$; $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$ $-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d69105-ab2d-55c3-9612-ee987e634694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8b9ffd-965a-5c5b-8676-57da40fbb9a9" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31720ab0-69d4-52f5-b3cb-fcd1e2ec0d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467258} {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b86baf-0e5a-524c-b103-8dac5824fae3" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} {:answer/id #uuid "6239f5bd-e810-5e80-93e2-7e27a749a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5a3574-1760-5b93-87fd-b5f37a0870f3" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ $-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"} :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441789} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441796} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"} :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482797} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2bf6a00-7292-5076-bf55-564c193a7119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467262} {:answer/id #uuid "436ad721-52a7-5bbb-9fec-c6b48f3ba98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "436ad721-52a7-5bbb-9fec-c6b48f3ba98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467263} :answer-field/id #uuid "423c55a3-60b4-51ca-b890-68935ec744be" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e5ac3-9d96-5080-8148-349a986d21c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467264} {:answer/id #uuid "912fb8dd-190c-5bb0-9707-6a05d1b9270e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912fb8dd-190c-5bb0-9707-6a05d1b9270e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467265} :answer-field/id #uuid "be581d5e-34c7-545f-8c9d-e0083c9b240d" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc722f86-30ee-5151-b020-c48679adb40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467266} {:answer/id #uuid "5193ed3d-e792-5b3e-afb8-99ca596974e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc722f86-30ee-5151-b020-c48679adb40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467266} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a8ad7e2-2970-535b-a09b-b090805a431e" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3afec700-f2aa-5108-b688-bd4de9c948ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-343756" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solutions $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ and $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$; $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :que