wer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467272} {:answer/id #uuid "b5910d95-689b-5498-970e-cb215a933405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5910d95-689b-5498-970e-cb215a933405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467273} :answer-field/id #uuid "1174ed98-32d3-5eef-b1f3-eb94a6edeefc" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceda7f80-0812-553b-8a96-78a8d37d4fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$, $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$, and $y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$; $y$ $=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$; $y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ $-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"} :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442732} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442743}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e62c72-eded-5251-b82c-a13d292823c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467274} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb19eae-ca8e-5045-ac0b-02fb0e26ab98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fb19eae-ca8e-5045-ac0b-02fb0e26ab98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467275} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a5b1b5-2edc-57f9-8e94-8a8cfee97bf6" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb