ch of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\\\\\,1<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/id #uuid "36521b0a-e13c-5296-b2d4-078f2470ad6d" :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdb0af4-bec9-5365-971f-ab546cd82d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467908} {:answer/id #uuid "d80b7404-e065-5db5-bcce-93cc1aebc4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fdb0af4-bec9-5365-971f-ab546cd82d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467908} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f8ce43-df76-5fc6-89d1-78a9a1e48f97" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468675} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3564aa95-c89d-5d78-b74c-425d2b171124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467910} {:answer/id #uuid "18c2174c-460e-5990-bc37-38b8de7a5f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3564aa95-c89d-5d78-b74c-425d2b171124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467910} :answer-field/id #uuid "2506e38a-e7c8-5102-ae2e-637f98ec3c36" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e879d1-636a-58da-87a1-9d0e2524a6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467912} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9a4911-e986-55a6-a8fd-43e12eed4d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9a4911-e986-55a6-a8fd-43e12eed4d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467913} :answer-field/id #uuid "046d1d39-39e9-58b5-8e61-b97eee9247b1" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2073d8ee-8ecd-594e-9fd2-e836c959a0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72130" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $1$ to $2$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $\\frac{1}{2}$ to $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/id #uuid "36521b0a-e13c-5296-b2d4-078f2470ad6d" :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b78cd2-bfb7-5330-aa6f-f7ce269e6b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467914} {:answer/id #uuid "27a8076d-8705-5d50-a53c-95dab2e496b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a8076d-8705-5d50-a53c-95dab2e496b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467915} :answer-field/id #uuid "d454bd2e-767a-5f21-944b-ed804dfff5fb" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bcc390e-ef7a-55fd-9a12-5d600f7acf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467916} {:answer/id #uuid "d2164cbf-cf1d-578d-80b9-b112dd5bfb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2164cbf-cf1d-578d-80b9-b112dd5bfb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467917} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce312647-eb92-5dce-aa4c-9426b435bac5" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cebe6bcc-6e83-516f-a0d5-c7fdae04f696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467918} {:answer/id #uuid "9baf9625-6765-5ea4-92b5-cb80c7acefbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cebe6bcc-6e83-516f-a0d5-c7fdae04f696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467918} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a3b488-fd9e-5da4-9e1a-9d233a047903" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4009c26-3316-54ec-b0bf-1d56ecb8b0b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72175" :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le y\\le 2}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We notice that:  \n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $\\sqrt{2}$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $-\\sqrt{3}$ to $2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$; ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5b2d85-4151-54a4-ac97-beef0024d1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468076} {:answer/id #uuid "2c392c8d-2089-591c-a70b-c5983cd9d8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5b2d85-4151-54a4-ac97-beef0024d1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468076} :answer-field/id #uuid "d486dc1c-3d30-5e4f-835e-f08717f52889" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\1-x-y=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465a6071-09f7-5ac0-928d-43e875a45ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468078} {:answer/id #uuid "43d1ede4-b752-517f-859b-1a7a07ade02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465a6071-09f7-5ac0-928d-43e875a45ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468078} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c858f4-cbfb-5002-a626-7d8e4c6fc2c6" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b2f350-f63b-50a8-a1f2-5bd05dffb414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468080} {:answer/id #uuid "f07e9305-0844-5a74-9550-81a8ec3521c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f07e9305-0844-5a74-9550-81a8ec3521c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa3dc22-e26d-5b76-84a3-23639eb52190" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\2x+3y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\2x+3y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"} :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434588} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434600}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4ee9b9a-eb51-5e15-abb2-05bb1b86b139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468082} {:answer/id #uuid "f22d2656-8795-5d89-ac40-9e4cae3d15a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ee9b9a-eb51-5e15-abb2-05bb1b86b139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468082} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e0eb83-eda5-5211-bd02-730bf01aa552" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d206fe7-45bb-5c68-ba7e-b82cba496bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468084} {:answer/id #uuid "e417d014-1256-54c2-b5a6-83c0e16db7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d206fe7-45bb-5c68-ba7e-b82cba496bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468084} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a030342-3365-5302-be22-c12324e628cc" :answer-field/key "$B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468763} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef52cd4-5c7a-5343-89cd-f60bf03a78bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468086} {:answer/id #uuid "993adad2-e813-5acf-9d8c-0d5673935db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef52cd4-5c7a-5343-89cd-f60bf03a78bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468086} :answer-field/id #uuid "e56f414b-9b1b-5b30-aeec-c4089b6d4315" :answer-field/key "$C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5037faf6-6f8a-5ea2-800f-2c136a3bb59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74218" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $a=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\na+b=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99a152a-4432-58e7-8095-7a6594ae2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468088} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1282bf-bb72-590a-82cb-57afddffd291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99a152a-4432-58e7-8095-7a6594ae2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf27b0a-4178-50bb-a563-2416eaa07855" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75291599-266c-544e-b704-be929998cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468090} {:answer/id #uuid "cf437d27-edd1-584d-be5b-b58d1bf9be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf437d27-edd1-584d-be5b-b58d1bf9be5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468091} :answer-field/id #uuid "11bc94d4-2ccf-5ec6-bfea-1ec2f439dda1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5x-y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90497ddb-e287-5539-b1a1-cd58a7c31e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468092} {:answer/id #uuid "80442660-0495-5466-89ce-25cccd762bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90497ddb-e287-5539-b1a1-cd58a7c31e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468092} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc40e59a-282b-5ffd-8ff6-51c6739169ba" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5-x-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\\\5x-y-z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85518922-a8e2-5430-be49-fc5bd9618d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451723} {:answer/id #uuid "32d23fcd-416f-5d3c-bfbd-0efc26263051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32d23fcd-416f-5d3c-bfbd-0efc26263051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6feab46e-0164-581d-b9ac-417f088f9a3e" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153dcd4c-f868-5cb9-9348-c787feeb008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451725} {:answer/id #uuid "db92ed10-887e-5be5-8e25-a589761415a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db92ed10-887e-5be5-8e25-a589761415a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dae3873-2189-5c0f-a6cd-a4ed4645e74e" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8f64e6-edb1-5d35-982e-d14dca05b3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ffa827-33a1-5d4c-86a0-6a5b242d8e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8f64e6-edb1-5d35-982e-d14dca05b3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7b874c-9786-5d4f-b21e-8d93eb56af6a" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})$ $=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64e956c1-f323-5e83-8c0c-e6640c910184" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$” is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$ is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})$ $=5$ is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$”; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"} :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424581} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424599}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526 :topic/title "Forced Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e55b64d-ca59-5b1b-8132-480d5e5c2162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469062} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7eb601-a102-57eb-ab76-3368d53d246a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e55b64d-ca59-5b1b-8132-480d5e5c2162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469062} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bbcaaf-48f8-5300-9d6c-30f93535d5bb" :answer-field/key "$y$ $={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)$ $+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d2c61d2-ea96-5e2c-84c0-d4d24f84b8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469064} {:answer/id #uuid "5a045206-d21e-58b1-8301-9ae8158a4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2c61d2-ea96-5e2c-84c0-d4d24f84b8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469064} :answer-field/id #uuid "d967e521-443b-500a-bc19-2c09a8121909" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$ $+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ $+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fdb81c-f0d6-5fb4-82bc-c7ea82119645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469066} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3855ff-b34d-5a72-8c94-208a381a9b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3855ff-b34d-5a72-8c94-208a381a9b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469067} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a327517-af88-54d5-891a-3fa107e65f62" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ $+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41288342-236a-5f59-a0ab-b7381746dd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79575" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $y$ $=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ $+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y$ $=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$ $+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ $+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526 :topic/title "Forced Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7e9294-2691-59db-86db-e95ddd85ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469074} {:answer/id #uuid "6e468d2c-aa10-5901-8140-c297d2b801f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7e9294-2691-59db-86db-e95ddd85ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469074} :answer-field/id #uuid "7010c678-eb0d-5608-8848-e2254c4eca81" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c2dd80-2cb3-547e-b24c-d098627d3608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469076} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb26764-4f36-5604-a364-c2f93bc6807a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb26764-4f36-5604-a364-c2f93bc6807a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469077} :answer-field/id #uuid "7497790b-5ec6-541d-a860-971fde22a604" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38aa6a3a-057a-531b-9597-683e74e5410a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469078} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2e0067-7e4a-5f82-9e30-a886fd6c0b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38aa6a3a-057a-531b-9597-683e74e5410a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469078} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b319f4e-bcbd-5749-accf-c11043839177" :answer-field/key "$y$ $={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)$ $+\\frac{1}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b57b422d-c4f7-5a90-ae50-c581e716c943" :question/math-academy-id "q-79586" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a constant, we assume that the particular solution is also a constant,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nOnly $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$ match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d4722f-ca99-5a82-9a0e-d4df7cc199ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469188} {:answer/id #uuid "86d93ef6-b705-5b2f-9377-64be6529a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d4722f-ca99-5a82-9a0e-d4df7cc199ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469188} :answer-field/id #uuid "139fbd62-63d3-5ea0-9351-3722ad6548ac" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470206} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f63149d3-8562-5f24-b3a5-83e61eff08fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469190} {:answer/id #uuid "869eb181-f5c4-59ff-9f16-a0d66e4a1f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869eb181-f5c4-59ff-9f16-a0d66e4a1f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469191} :answer-field/id #uuid "78abad7e-43e2-5c5a-be47-562bf6033e7e" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e8e86e-e05a-54da-ae84-5b1c51c4d927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469192} {:answer/id #uuid "3009167a-891e-5dd7-b8d3-5d7917af69b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48e8e86e-e05a-54da-ae84-5b1c51c4d927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469192} :answer-field/id #uuid "94af2aeb-4d6f-5c6b-8dbf-c756c480cba7" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63f1d1b0-1722-552b-83dc-40f0511e40cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80566" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d92fdb93f7564e1c205ab8603945eb6704c03db92c46130810e2b7cbce9a0e20.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cac20f8-0c6e-5c22-96f1-a90b74e7288e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451783} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d918d-056c-5cbe-8563-756d169831c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af9d918d-056c-5cbe-8563-756d169831c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451784} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1801346-2b7f-5c0e-bf2e-14d91b77c360" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1183a-c815-569d-9d59-4ac828f44466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451785} {:answer/id #uuid "230098a1-56a8-5b70-85a0-677dd17b3b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c1183a-c815-569d-9d59-4ac828f44466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451785} :answer-field/id #uuid "850131e8-3e45-5741-ab52-00ec54ad3707" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76bb12c3-fa36-50ea-a3b0-43561a75e8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451787} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2a906b-4563-5dbb-9794-82a69e0005c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2a906b-4563-5dbb-9794-82a69e0005c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451788} :answer-field/id #uuid "32afa441-a36d-5b70-a78f-f611e0661706" :answer-field/key "${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87eecbfe-e80e-5243-96ba-7676172d9f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-80567" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}-4$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}3x-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/a7/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$”; “${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481765} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482634}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} {:answer/id #uuid "34be447a-67c4-5a73-8480-4c9e222a6cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cbfe12-d294-5245-8577-5393288ad9f4" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b396de7c-837a-5a29-89f1-49cbd1530aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469208} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af40fc-fffa-522e-a59f-f12c36507ded" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf7232-c17f-5a4f-aed3-4cd3d5ebaa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469210} {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6521383-885b-5c99-af14-d69ae9a433ee" :answer-field/key "${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ $=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy$ $+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy$ $+2xy$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$” and “${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ $=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy$ $+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy$ $+2xy$” only'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409637} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481568} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac5898e-ccd9-5404-8efc-311f58a28a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438598} {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599} :answer-field/id #uuid "6578c37d-0b49-5206-b245-6ec044a9e0dd" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec262c2d-cd4c-5f8a-9d3f-9bbc45bf6356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438600} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb8753c-ab98-5e82-82cd-ee42afcd9f28" :answer-field/key "$G(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i$ $+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3d211e-4ac3-517a-add9-2d442df1b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438602} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0eaf1f-730a-5a51-9bf3-fa77e2cb6d36" :answer-field/key "$H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc1a89db-98a6-54e9-a27c-1ebc3462a7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81460" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$; $G(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i$ $+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$; $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} {:answer/id #uuid "aca9ec9d-b6b0-5219-90db-431dcf2f158f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dda7980-1744-5da6-8443-f5cd3aa61fb8" :answer-field/key "$F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff09ca98-e109-5e9b-a23c-9174cdfe3d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438606} {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6a548e-e667-5d89-9d10-459ef9e2e419" :answer-field/key "$G$ $=(2x+1)\\,i$ $+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47704e-68c1-5d9e-9f9b-802de105632e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd11437-4a71-571d-b489-a706ff584c35" :answer-field/key "$H={y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2410b4fd-4172-5cce-b672-b1259e48aa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81743" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x+1$ and $Q=5{y}^{2}+2y+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G$ $=(2x+1)\\,i$ $+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526 :topic/title "Forced Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ea5463-bea1-5eaf-bc15-3836a5ba47fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469698} {:answer/id #uuid "07fd1def-dfaa-5ec4-ae66-bd968103e02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07fd1def-dfaa-5ec4-ae66-bd968103e02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469699} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c1229eb-a220-5d64-9dde-8d4198484535" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470461} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e48f9942-dc1d-5bfd-8c7e-fffcfc58f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469700} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7c7638-d189-5ef7-8aee-3f80c8472788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e48f9942-dc1d-5bfd-8c7e-fffcfc58f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469700} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f4b973-4fd4-5ee7-97b7-671e55562e4d" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c12017-b6ff-5bed-a4ab-944b10eb21be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469702} {:answer/id #uuid "62d36b84-29c6-5fca-991c-d87a064eec38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c12017-b6ff-5bed-a4ab-944b10eb21be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469702} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef40f75-93cb-5ffc-a30d-cf9bc3956155" :answer-field/key "$y$ $=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ $-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ $+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feaf4026-120d-5dc5-a147-ce0975029f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-94376" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526 :topic/title "Forced Oscillators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cae621f-3d6e-5d45-9b65-1fad41f795f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469716} {:answer/id #uuid "21ad5055-2b76-5888-b77e-0849f5f11837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ad5055-2b76-5888-b77e-0849f5f11837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed908e42-450b-5534-9da1-729e23bf4ae6" :answer-field/key "$y$ $={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)$ $+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc52e9e4-97b0-5304-be16-59ad68d87273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469718} {:answer/id #uuid "8278a36c-ae51-5440-b66c-2b9da7b262a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc52e9e4-97b0-5304-be16-59ad68d87273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469718} :answer-field/id #uuid "1847518c-ebde-5827-8e28-1cc95c28b40a" :answer-field/key "$y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44e8621-e0a4-5954-b461-751df8afdcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469720} {:answer/id #uuid "60960cba-b7ee-5e40-a9ae-22c295ee5156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44e8621-e0a4-5954-b461-751df8afdcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469720} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f659e9-9050-529d-929a-3c5358c2751f" :answer-field/key "$y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+10{e}^{t}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf314c8-ddb9-53cd-832f-d87f0d4562fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94383" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=10{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $y$ $={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)$ $+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$ matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y$ $={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)$ $+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902db5be-70c1-5674-b997-99068ce5edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452029} {:answer/id #uuid "814a1883-467d-5324-b6ef-116433356eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "814a1883-467d-5324-b6ef-116433356eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452030} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c08151d-a778-5873-a9ce-fddc4ab45b26" :answer-field/key "The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7a65cf4-7f49-5de1-8fe2-89baa7786a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452031} {:answer/id #uuid "e089eec9-7866-5f1a-a19f-c3d7e6029a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a65cf4-7f49-5de1-8fe2-89baa7786a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452031} :answer-field/id #uuid "f698df9a-9c04-5d46-ad26-9bbf7397619f" :answer-field/key "For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe2c7-ade2-5481-b820-bfe9f428123f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452033} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff2aef1-031d-5390-a56e-ad3f1cd32bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe2c7-ade2-5481-b820-bfe9f428123f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452033} :answer-field/id #uuid "b85432db-4f8a-59af-af6d-ee5723b311ce" :answer-field/key "There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)$ $\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86d246c9-ffba-5c6c-b83e-58058d87e01e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94686" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2}+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$” is true. Indeed, the constant polynomial $1$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n1=0\\cdot {x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nHence, $1\\in E.$\n\n- Statement “For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$” is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n1+p(x)=1+{x}^{2}+1={x}^{2}+2\\ne p(x).\n$$\n\nThe zero-polynomial $0$ is the unique polynomial such that for any $p(x)\\in E$ we have $0+p(x)=p(x).$\n\n- Statement “There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)$ $\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$” is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in E,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a3e2-e736-5b19-b171-2993227de350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452041} {:answer/id #uuid "9b98079f-fe90-5caa-80dc-7270db379b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1474a3e2-e736-5b19-b171-2993227de350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452041} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b953735-6549-5e74-ab38-2ec79639a675" :answer-field/key "For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))$ $=2p(x)$ $-2q(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ec80e9-0df8-5cfd-835c-fd48ded2c328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452043} {:answer/id #uuid "69a7ca61-3cc1-5e61-baf2-dce127884e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a7ca61-3cc1-5e61-baf2-dce127884e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452044} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2d98f2-45aa-5106-9dbe-0b4791dcbb31" :answer-field/key "For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9c42b9-1d20-519c-b18e-c231243717e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452045} {:answer/id #uuid "d0500db8-c267-5db0-b0d1-5a3a33dc76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0500db8-c267-5db0-b0d1-5a3a33dc76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452046} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50fa798-62df-5307-a327-7013e9729e7e" :answer-field/key "For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0778fc80-bc7a-5da8-b455-9670cb2d5afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94711" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))$ $=2p(x)$ $-2q(x).$” is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$”; “For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$” only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481574} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481750}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/id #uuid "02ecb448-e544-51fa-b80e-63ee84a4cef1" :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d1ca0c-c077-564f-bbd3-6bd589f331e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422351} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce1d0c-32be-5143-a872-7d49dc66b488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d1ca0c-c077-564f-bbd3-6bd589f331e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422351} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fca4796-70aa-50d1-96e1-5e660ddb7451" :answer-field/key "$2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5727dd59-2f56-5c06-9b43-b63ff7c2bfb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422353} {:answer/id #uuid "842ba921-5998-5c95-bb52-17e348e3161e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "842ba921-5998-5c95-bb52-17e348e3161e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422354} :answer-field/id #uuid "658f8908-07e3-5b79-8798-c03791ed181b" :answer-field/key "$3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8203d5-1002-51cb-9411-d4a83fc42ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422355} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4c0de1-0610-56fb-9005-f107985bbfef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8203d5-1002-51cb-9411-d4a83fc42ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422355} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff5351b-54cc-5ac7-889b-b6d3ef3ffe59" :answer-field/key "$2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}$ $={y}^{′},$ $\\,y(2)$ $=1,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765ad5be-eea7-5159-b982-461fb59d4587" :question/math-academy-id "q-96988" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}$ $={y}^{′},$ $\\,y(2)$ $=1,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(2)$ $=1$ is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/id #uuid "02ecb448-e544-51fa-b80e-63ee84a4cef1" :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc873da1-dcb5-58cd-bcdc-f6dfaecd44eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422357} {:answer/id #uuid "aabe49eb-f60e-59a4-8e8e-b51a643069e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabe49eb-f60e-59a4-8e8e-b51a643069e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422358} :answer-field/id #uuid "793806ab-841f-56f5-aae9-1f5aa1b28e93" :answer-field/key "${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e61410e-bb7d-5fd5-a42c-93728f72a519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422359} {:answer/id #uuid "3904d9fc-81e9-58dc-b100-87ee0a13dfc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e61410e-bb7d-5fd5-a42c-93728f72a519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422359} :answer-field/id #uuid "503ea99b-8961-55e4-a7e1-ce13e2130792" :answer-field/key "$\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}$ $=\\frac{y}{4},$ $\\,y(0)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(0)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{″}(0)$ $=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4347d9eb-3074-5e4f-bb59-94bee475caab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422361} {:answer/id #uuid "82b802ce-94eb-52a4-a016-7da0aee33a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82b802ce-94eb-52a4-a016-7da0aee33a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422362} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f981e7a-b93e-545d-9956-04d8aae8a018" :answer-field/key "$\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a63b835-35e3-55e6-8b41-bbb1ab5d0792" :question/math-academy-id "q-96990" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}$ $=\\frac{y}{4},$ $\\,y(0)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(0)$ $=2,$ $\\,{y}^{″}(0)$ $=1$ is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$; $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/id #uuid "1a7775b9-b31c-568f-86d9-182b4c190efc" :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f285c5-f28d-52d7-8edb-dc3cd9308f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451845} {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bbe03ec-cd1a-5f9e-bdaa-621344b8730a" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2821d1-dbdf-57db-8669-559772eabf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451847} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdec4e8-476e-5e64-bfe1-002886cf49b8" :answer-field/key "If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26320c-f5fc-5d22-962b-bc6fcc8e7c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451849} {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dc4bf6-46bb-5bfd-8850-b313313b3744" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" :question/math-academy-id "q-84063" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$” is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$”; “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$”; “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049324 :topic/id #uuid "ebff858d-86f0-5d5d-b497-f8fb2d20cce6" :topic/math-academy-id 2742 :topic/title "Introduction to Boundary Value Problems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537341a3-b43e-5de5-abbf-be0b40038210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468996} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc3237d-c01b-59bd-ba06-493cb3fa2223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc3237d-c01b-59bd-ba06-493cb3fa2223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468997} :answer-field/id #uuid "951286a1-a0b1-570b-a091-4d880a35417c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y$ $={x}^{2},$ $\\,y(0)$ $=1,$ $\\,y(2)$ $=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33c629e-3750-5cfe-859b-230f6599a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468998} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc63a8b-05c4-53d2-b7ff-a65dc9eb5a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33c629e-3750-5cfe-859b-230f6599a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468998} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fb633b-1980-597c-a228-197c17e0f800" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}-4y$ $=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ $\\,y(1)$ $=0,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab5ca09-5d8d-5b1a-b074-6ced4e9ed287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469000} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb3f62-a946-51ed-87c7-5b4787f5d785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab5ca09-5d8d-5b1a-b074-6ced4e9ed287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469000} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d485ac1-003d-50ae-bc31-a039db24bb63" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67ebcbc8-9a0b-5eef-bc58-dbb711498ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338536" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906 :topic/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeec09eb-e146-5cb6-9ecd-74e1373f6b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454160} {:answer/id #uuid "091ba662-f83a-54ea-8751-ba6dbe34e873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "091ba662-f83a-54ea-8751-ba6dbe34e873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454161} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b03dedf-81ed-54ca-abc8-ade7e68f51b5" :answer-field/key "For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d51a5b6-b7e4-5dc7-98b1-3114ece1d7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454162} {:answer/id #uuid "8b369da8-e845-5c29-bd67-b72cc8af8031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b369da8-e845-5c29-bd67-b72cc8af8031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454163} :answer-field/id #uuid "787ddf06-35b0-54d7-900d-849dec286111" :answer-field/key "For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee38294-5ead-5035-9335-de2045acce23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454164} {:answer/id #uuid "135f0f0d-316b-5739-bf19-d6c9661c2023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "135f0f0d-316b-5739-bf19-d6c9661c2023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454165} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a1ceb6c-f7ca-5769-8e6c-8e64058b055d" :answer-field/key "For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))$ $=\\alpha f(x)$ $+\\alpha g(x).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c4d0420-8d1b-51e3-ab5c-ec13f5c75afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94735" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a\\,\\text{ }{2}^{x},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement “For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))$ $=\\alpha f(x)$ $+\\alpha g(x).$” is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in E$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “For any $f(x)\\in E,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$”; “For any $f(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$”; “For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))$ $=\\alpha f(x)$ $+\\alpha g(x).$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc613be0-2d34-5253-8fbe-7d2a5fcd0840" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cases Where a Stream Function Exists" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/id #uuid "04ff54ad-5d9d-5751-8a07-6602f0f05410" :topic/math-academy-id 3717 :topic/title "Stream Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4366cf07-5267-5553-b95a-477dd2d2babf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471155} {:answer/id #uuid "07743ed4-e0cf-5a80-98a1-bf0ff80adbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4366cf07-5267-5553-b95a-477dd2d2babf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471155} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa258509-4376-5dea-a87e-a693b9267a9e" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=y{e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e283333-f3e0-5703-a7d6-b18f26818a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471157} {:answer/id #uuid "daa5cbcd-922c-5e9f-81df-331d42b25c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa5cbcd-922c-5e9f-81df-331d42b25c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fafe627-603d-589b-9e5f-fed09d470e45" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e3a767-597c-5282-929f-213f0fc71f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471159} {:answer/id #uuid "92c089da-6cd9-54a0-bead-4915120a621e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e3a767-597c-5282-929f-213f0fc71f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471159} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ae356f-e168-5c92-a787-fde7297cecf7" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-2y\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28d6fde6-f5ac-58b4-8494-7d66bda4ef81" :question/math-academy-id "q-162616" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y{e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{x}+x{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ has a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-2y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2xy+(-2)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e476a7e6-9fe8-5985-ab3d-474e04a7980c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470829} {:answer/id #uuid "7af1392e-9bfd-5650-b6c7-cc5983449290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e476a7e6-9fe8-5985-ab3d-474e04a7980c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470829} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa6ab55-2c08-50d9-8d9e-4dda3b96a9e4" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }3x+y<8,\\\\\\,0<x<3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbbc87e-3691-52f5-8ce4-c5cbaad6b748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470831} {:answer/id #uuid "7e31bb92-ac20-5fe1-8af7-bc33a8b119a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e31bb92-ac20-5fe1-8af7-bc33a8b119a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470832} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e97e70a-fdb0-5789-bc5d-9049a1c4f9c7" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\\\\\,2<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a2cecd-883c-5851-be4b-db3c4e83d69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470833} {:answer/id #uuid "2b94ed74-766b-54f1-92b2-f5c251317f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a2cecd-883c-5851-be4b-db3c4e83d69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470833} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b3b63f-64f6-53e3-a996-0684f567a58b" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<6\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c082af6c-4b35-55f0-b925-7876243430ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-105443" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\\\\\,2<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3f425a-0a1f-515b-8c03-990715e116f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470835} {:answer/id #uuid "46c61006-9d69-5224-84b5-adb4594d575a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46c61006-9d69-5224-84b5-adb4594d575a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470836} :answer-field/id #uuid "c24fe181-c890-5457-b487-5bd9845a29a6" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,6\\le x<9,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455ea2d6-36ea-506e-8abc-4fb2725d1508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470837} {:answer/id #uuid "b1bbc0e3-f77f-5075-b7c1-c24869a40657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "455ea2d6-36ea-506e-8abc-4fb2725d1508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470837} :answer-field/id #uuid "523aebc4-d33b-5507-8bcd-9a008fe77b20" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53fe6cb-2b4e-5db2-a7b8-cf233dd5a07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470839} {:answer/id #uuid "0c743f3a-ed2f-55ac-b5d2-1af41de6baef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a53fe6cb-2b4e-5db2-a7b8-cf233dd5a07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470839} :answer-field/id #uuid "39ebafac-d103-5f82-9a2a-34326808b7f4" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}<3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f4b0cdf-9fe1-57d6-a009-4edf0331d401" :question/math-academy-id "q-105444" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(6,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(8,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,6\\le x<9,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16604ddb-69ca-5ace-9dbf-0383a82ea98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471113} {:answer/id #uuid "ed093c42-08ca-5342-9c3d-5d707ac0618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16604ddb-69ca-5ace-9dbf-0383a82ea98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471113} :answer-field/id #uuid "271f6907-9f07-58b0-b69e-9138291c3f29" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0<x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be1298c-3ffd-5e63-bdaf-4b61ccf3b37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471115} {:answer/id #uuid "257b2998-d432-57cf-9773-e11354e3ef90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "257b2998-d432-57cf-9773-e11354e3ef90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471116} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c83fa2d-fc72-5a50-960b-e7f3fe4261ae" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae39a58d-0d80-5e95-a397-418e9ba63826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471117} {:answer/id #uuid "974db8f3-030a-560c-ab7c-a6b736fbe846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974db8f3-030a-560c-ab7c-a6b736fbe846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471118} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5293d9-c7c0-526b-9f08-83692bebc37d" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28c149d9-63e5-5d01-abf9-5e8569624b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161639" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7adf2c3e-4a73-5098-ab60-790d637728cf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Open and Closed Sets in the Plane in Set Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 :topic/id #uuid "9f807cd5-8888-510e-98d9-a7920b0d7b50" :topic/math-academy-id 4097 :topic/title "Open and Closed Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7519ae6-6283-5e9b-a86d-15f672cad5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471119} {:answer/id #uuid "18729287-882a-5576-aa4a-14fe49a63438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18729287-882a-5576-aa4a-14fe49a63438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471120} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c5d9db-d0d7-59d7-a450-9db137467fd8" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}\\lef