order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(3)$ $=-5$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=-5.$\n\n- System $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(4)$ $=5$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=5.$\n\n- System $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x}$ is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(3)$ $=-5$; $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-5y\\right)+x{y}^{′}$ $={e}^{x},$ $\\,y(4)$ $=5$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412 :topic/id #uuid "46db30fb-cff1-5fef-85ad-641fbb5c68f2" :topic/math-academy-id 3343 :topic/title "Vector Fields"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aab6b82-5473-54b2-8342-d5b12a067309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567189} {: